WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.780
The tallest measured coast redwoods stand more than 100 metres high.

2
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:13.080
Every leaf at the top gets water from roots in the soil.

3
00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.520
A tree has no heart and no pump.

4
00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.900
So what lifts the water?

5
00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.620
The answer is the dry air around the leaves.

6
00:00:23.533 --> 00:00:27.313
Water potential predicts which way water moves.

7
00:00:28.353 --> 00:00:31.673
Pure water at atmospheric pressure is defined as zero.

8
00:00:32.613 --> 00:00:36.193
Water always moves toward lower total water potential.

9
00:00:37.133 --> 00:00:38.433
Take illustrative values.

10
00:00:39.513 --> 00:00:41.653
Soil is minus 0.3.

11
00:00:42.713 --> 00:00:44.633
Root is minus 0.6.

12
00:00:45.673 --> 00:00:47.553
Leaf is minus 1.5.

13
00:00:47.553 --> 00:00:52.453
Air at 50% relative humidity is about minus 94.

14
00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:55.233
Every step runs downhill.

15
00:00:55.993 --> 00:00:58.353
The biggest drop is from leaf to air.

16
00:00:59.217 --> 00:01:05.617
Inside a leaf, water evaporates from the wet walls of the inner photosynthetic

17
00:01:05.617 --> 00:01:11.637
cells. The water left in the walls is held more tightly, and its pressure falls.

18
00:01:12.837 --> 00:01:14.757
Water moves in from the xylem.

19
00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:22.117
Xylem cells that conduct are dead and hollow, a continuous pathway from root to

20
00:01:22.117 --> 00:01:29.457
leaf. Cohesion, the attraction between water molecules, lets a thin column in a

21
00:01:29.457 --> 00:01:31.797
narrow tube be pulled like a rope.

22
00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.310
Gravity sets a floor on the pull.

23
00:01:36.430 --> 00:01:42.070
Water potential rises by about 0.01 megapascals for each metre of height.

24
00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:50.070
A leaf 30 metres up needs 0.30 megapascals of extra tension just to hold a still

25
00:01:50.070 --> 00:01:52.070
column, before any flow.

26
00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:59.150
At the top of a 110-metre redwood, gravity alone adds over 1 megapascal.

27
00:01:59.983 --> 00:02:02.823
Could the roots push water up instead?

28
00:02:03.783 --> 00:02:06.303
Where measured, root pressure is small.

29
00:02:07.023 --> 00:02:15.015
The highest value in a survey of 109 tropical vines and woody species was about 0.15 MPa.

30
00:02:15.115 --> 00:02:20.403
That is enough in principle to raise water about 15 metres.

31
00:02:21.323 --> 00:02:24.503
It is weak or absent when transpiration is fastest.

32
00:02:25.243 --> 00:02:29.643
Cut the xylem of a transpiring tree, and air is drawn in.

33
00:02:30.463 --> 00:02:31.583
Sap does not spurt out.

34
00:02:32.383 --> 00:02:37.483
A vertebrate heart pushes blood round a closed loop at positive pressure.

35
00:02:38.143 --> 00:02:45.380
A typical peak arterial pressure in a resting adult human is about 0.016 megapascals.

36
00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:53.043
In a transpiring plant, xylem water moves one way, root to leaf, under negative pressure.

37
00:02:53.863 --> 00:02:55.343
Evaporation powers it.

38
00:02:55.343 --> 00:02:58.383
Xylem water is pulled, not pushed.
